  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 301 AM CDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nThe leading edge of the showers that are expected to move across the \nregion today are stretching from northwestern Minnesota through the \neastern portions of North and South Dakota and into northern \nNebraska. These showers will move west to east across the area today \nas a trough moves across southern Canada with a surface low spinning \nup underneath, in addition to a mid level occlusion in the \nsouthwestern US. A broad area of lift is being observed between the \ntwo, resulting in a broad line of weak to moderate rain showers with \nsome rumbles of thunder over North Dakota this morning. The strength \nof showers is expected to slowly increase as low level frontogenesis \nenhanced by WAA ramps up between the two aforementioned systems, \nwith rain amounts increasing towards the later part of today and \ninto Wednesday. Areas of western MN where the rain is arriving this \nmorning can expect to see around a quarter inch up to a half inch at \nmost. Amounts increase to around a half inch in eastern MN including \nthe Twin Cities metro, and the highest amounts will push farther \nsouth still as the boundary stalls due to slowing motion of the \nCanadian branch of the system, with the position of the stationary \nfront expected to settle over northern Iowa and into southeastern \nMinnesota. The position of the stationary boundary will be the \nlocations that see the greatest chance for training rain showers and \ntherefore localized flooding, and at least right now appears most \nlikely along I-90 in southern MN as depicted by the low level wind \nvectors within the various guidance. The NBM has continued to tone \ndown the overall amount of QPF this far north, instead favoring the \nareas just to the south of the MPX coverage area for the amounts \nover an inch and even beyond that.\n\nSubsidence increases by the end of the week as a surface high slides \ninto the northern plains on the heels of the departing system, \nresulting in dry conditions and near normal temperatures to end the \nweek and into the weekend. Cooler temperatures are expected at night \nwith lows dropping into the 40s by early next week, however not \nenough to consider frost being a possibility just yet. Ensemble \nguidance shows little in the way of additional precipitation chances \nwith a small chance on Sunday, however the better environment \nremains farther to the north where a trough slides across southern \nCanada. Our average temperatures continue to decrease especially now \nthat we are past the autumn equinox, with minutes of daylight being \nlost more quickly now. We will keep an eye on frost chances, but for \nnow they are beyond the traditional 7 day forecast with the colder \nair still locked up north.",
